   "l": "The great state — lying low, the down-flow,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "A great state lies lowest, where the rivers run down,",
   "n": "Five characters, no verb, no 'is'. 者 hangs 大國 up as the topic and 下流 drops after it as the comment: 'the great state — low-lying, the downstream'. English cannot leave the gap open and has to put something in it.\n\nWang Bi supplies the picture: rivers and seas lie lowest of all, and so the hundred streams run down into them; a great state that places itself low has all under heaven run to it in the same way. 下 is this chapter's keyword and turns up nine times in the chapter's ninety-seven characters — sometimes 'low', sometimes the verb 'to put oneself below'. Watch it move.",
   "p": true
