   "l": "the small state, by going below the great state,\nthen takes the great state.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "and a small state, by going below a great state, gains the great state.",
   "n": "The two halves are word-for-word symmetrical, but 取 cannot mean quite the same thing twice. Wang Bi reads the first as the great state gathering the small in and the second as the small state attaching itself and being taken in — one 取 is absorption, the other is acceptance. Classical Chinese lets a single verb face both ways because nothing in the grammar marks which side of the transaction the subject is on."
