   "l": "Therefore one puts itself below so as to take;\none puts itself below, and takes.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "So one goes below in order to take; the other goes below, and takes all the same.",
   "n": "The whole weight of the couplet rests on two particles. 以 makes the lowering a chosen means — you go under in order to get. 而 only strings the two verbs together — you go under, and the getting follows. Everything else in the two lines is identical.\n\nWhich state is which is not stated, and the text refuses to say. Read with the lines before it, the deliberate one is the great state and the incidental one the small; read the other way round, the small state is the calculating one. Both have been argued, and nothing in Wang Bi settles it."
