   "l": "The great state wants no more than to take men in together and rear them;\nthe small state wants no more than to enter and serve men.\nNow the two of them each get what they desire —\nthe greater one ought to make itself low.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "A great state wants no more than to gather others in and feed them; a small state wants no more than to enter another's service. Both get what they want — and it is the greater that ought to put itself below.",
   "n": "事 is the noun 'an affair, business to be handled' doing duty as a verb, 'to be somebody's business, to serve him'. Classical Chinese has no word-classes fixed in advance: the character is the word, and its job comes from where it stands. You will meet this constantly.\n\nBoth of the first two lines end on 人 — the same word rhyming with itself, which Chinese verse permits and English will not.\n\nThe last line is the chapter's whole politics: the argument has been even-handed all the way through, and then the obligation is laid on the strong party only. 宜 is a mild word, 'it would be fitting', not a command."
  }
